<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1871 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Internationale</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fr">L'Internationale</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French song of 1871 that became the anthem of workers' and socialist movements and was sung in many languages around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Eugène Pottier</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Pierre De Geyter</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>socialist, communist, anarchist and social democratic workers' movements</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Paris, France (the words, 1871); Lille, France (the music, 1888)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words June 1871 (an earlier version September 1870); music 1888, first performed in Lille in July 1888</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Eugène Pottier (d. 1887); music by Pierre De Geyter (d. 1932), public domain in France since October 2014)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Eugène Pottier, a member of the First International who had attended its congress of 1864 and a member of the Paris Commune, wrote the French words in June 1871, after the French army crushed the Commune on 28 May and before he fled to Britain and then, from 1873 to 1881, to the United States. He had written an earlier version in September 1870 to greet the Third Republic declared after the fall of the Second Empire, and meant the song to be sung to the tune of &quot;La Marseillaise&quot;. The text that endures is the one Pottier authorised for his Chants révolutionnaires, published by his fellow Communard Jean Allemane in April 1887; Pottier died in November that year, and the song was reputedly sung to the Marseillaise at his burial.</p><p>The melody now sung was composed the following year by Pierre De Geyter, a woodcarver and member of the French Workers' Party in the industrial city of Lille, for the party choir La Lyre des Travailleurs. He had been commissioned by Gustave Delory, later mayor of Lille, who had the text from a young socialist teacher, Charles Gros, and it was first performed in July 1888. To protect De Geyter's job, the first leaflets printed only the French form of his surname; a later edition named his brother Adolphe as composer. Pierre lost a lawsuit over the authorship in 1914 and was recognised only in 1922, aged 74; Adolphe, who died by suicide in 1916, left a note saying that Delory had manipulated him into the claim. The song's success went with that of the Second International, which adopted it as its official anthem, and by the 1910 International Socialist Congress in Copenhagen it had been sung in 18 languages.</p><p>Translations carried it across the world and often changed it. Charles Hope Kerr published the first American version in 1894, after hearing the song in Lille; Emil Luckhardt wrote the best-known German text in 1896 for Wilhelm Liebknecht; Arkady Kots made the Russian version in 1902, which was reworded after the Bolshevik Revolution. The Soviet Union used the song as its national anthem until it was replaced by the &quot;State Anthem of the USSR&quot;. In China Qu Qiubai retranslated it from the French in 1923, and from that year it has closed every National Congress of the Chinese Communist Party; accounts of the deaths of Li Dazhao in 1927 and of later Communist martyrs tell that they sang it, and it became a leitmotif of Chinese revolutionary cinema. Kazi Nazrul Islam translated it into Bengali around 1927, Ho Chi Minh into Vietnamese, Abolqasem Lahouti into Persian, Yeghishe Charents into Armenian; it was sung by workers in South Africa until the Communist Party was suppressed from 1950, and returned on South African radio in Zulu in 1990.</p><p>In the Second World War the song became a matter of diplomacy. After Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941, Winston Churchill forbade the BBC to play it in its weekly programme of Allied national anthems; the BBC dropped the programme rather than go on leaving it out, and Churchill lifted the ban on 22 January 1942. In 1943 the BBC's Salute to the Red Army brought a mass performance to the Royal Albert Hall, and Arturo Toscanini insisted on including his orchestration of the song in the 1944 film Hymn of the Nations; during the McCarthy era it was cut from re-released copies until a 1988 Library of Congress release restored it.</p><p>The song has served both governments and those who opposed them: students sang it in their final retreat from Tiananmen Square in 1989. It is still sung by communist, socialist and labour movements, and new translations continue to appear, among them a Swahili version adopted by a Kenyan party in November 2024.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the poor and exploited workers of every country to rise, unite and take the world into their own hands. It rejects waiting for any saviour from above and holds that working people must free themselves. Its mood is defiant and confident of a coming victory.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Debout ! les damnés de la terre !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Debout ! les forçats de la faim !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La raison tonne en son cratère :</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est l’éruption de la fin.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Du passé faisons table rase,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Foule esclave, debout ! debout !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le monde va changer de base :</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous ne sommes rien, soyons tout !</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il n’est pas de sauveurs suprêmes :</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ni Dieu, ni César, ni tribun,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Producteurs, sauvons-nous nous-mêmes !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Décrétons le salut commun !</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour que le voleur rende gorge,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour tirer l’esprit du cachot,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Soufflons nous-mêmes à notre forge,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Battons le fer quand il est chaud !</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 52 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’État comprime et la loi triche ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’Impôt saigne le malheureux ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nul devoir ne s’impose au riche ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le droit du pauvre est un mot creux.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est assez languir en tutelle,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’Égalité veut d’autres lois ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">« Pas de droits sans devoirs, dit-elle</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">« Égaux, pas de devoirs sans droits ! »</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Hideux dans leur apothéose,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les rois de la mine et du rail</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ont-ils jamais fait autre chose</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Que dévaliser le travail ?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dans les coffres-forts de la bande</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ce qu’il a créé s’est fondu</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">En décrétant qu’on le lui rende</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le peuple ne veut que son dû.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Les Rois nous soûlaient de fumées,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Paix entre nous, guerre aux tyrans !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Appliquons la grève aux armées,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Crosse en l’air, et rompons les rangs !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S’ils s’obstinent, ces cannibales,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À faire de nous des héros,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ils sauront bientôt que nos balles</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sont pour nos propres généraux.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ouvriers, paysans, nous sommes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le grand parti des travailleurs ;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La terre n’appartient qu’aux hommes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’oisif ira loger ailleurs.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Combien de nos chairs se repaissent !</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais, si les corbeaux, les vautours,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Un de ces matins, disparaissent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le soleil brillera toujours !</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est la lutte finale :</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Groupons-nous, et demain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’Internationale</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sera le genre humain</span></p></div></details></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arise, ye pris'ners of starvation!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arise, ye wretched of the earth!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>For justice thunders condemnation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>A better world's in birth!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No more tradition's chains shall bind us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Arise ye slaves, no more in thrall!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth shall rise on new foundations,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We have been naught, we shall be all.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Chorus:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Tis the final conflict,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let each stand in his place.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The International Party</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall be the human race.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>(Alternate chorus)</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'Tis the final conflict,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let each stand in their place.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The International Working Class</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall free the human race.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 54 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We want no condescending saviors</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To rule us from a judgment hall;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We workers ask not for their favors;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us consult for all.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To make the thief disgorge his booty</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To free the spirit from its cell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We must ourselves decide our duty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>We must decide, and do it well.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>(Chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The law oppresses us and tricks us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Taxation drains the victims’ blood;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The rich are free from obligations,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The laws the poor delude.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Too long we’ve languished in subjection,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Equality has other laws;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;No rights,&quot; says she &quot;without their duties,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No claims on equals without cause.&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>(Chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Behold them seated in their glory</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The kings of mine and rail and soil!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>What have you read in all their story,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>But how they plundered toil?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fruits of the people's work are buried</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the strong coffers of a few;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>In voting for their restitution</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The men will only ask their due.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>(Chorus)</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Toilers from shops and fields united,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The party we of all who work;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth belongs to us, the people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>No room here for the shirk.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>How many on our flesh have fattened;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>But if the noisome birds of prey</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shall vanish from the sky some morning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The blessed sunlight still will stay.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>(Chorus)</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Eugène Pottier (1871). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/L%E2%80%99Internationale" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Translated by Charles H. Kerr (1900). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Internationale_(Kerr)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="oaUAHUUoUsc" data-title="The Internationale: Marc Ogeret, in French"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Marc Ogeret, in French</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Marc Ogeret - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oaUAHUUoUsc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German version by Emil Luckhardt</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1896</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Russian version by Arkady Kots</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1902</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mystery-Bouffe (Vladimir Mayakovsky)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words as an &quot;Internationale of the Future&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Storming of the Winter Palace (Nikolai Evreinov)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1920</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chinese version by Qu Qiubai</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1923</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Girl Friends, film score (Dmitry Shostakovich)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">These Things Shall Be (John Ireland)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (the horn part; later removed)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">German version by Erich Weinert for the Thälmann Brigade</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hymn of the Nations, film (Arturo Toscanini's orchestration, in Verdi's Inno delle nazioni)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1944</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Animal Farm (George Orwell), the anthem &quot;Beasts of England&quot;</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Internationale (Billy Bragg)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1989</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hymn of the Golden Dawn</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar melody</span></li><li><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1935</span><span class="sg-chain-h">opening motif inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1792</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Pottier wrote the words to be sung to its tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pottier's earlier song for the Third Republic and the First International</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1870</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier version of the text</span></li></ul></section>
